# Lecture 14D

## Conditions for Hardy-Weinberg

## Chapter 10

## Dr. Neil Voss

## December 8, 2026

=== layout: bookend

# Learning Objectives

@body

- State the five Hardy-Weinberg conditions.
- Identify which conditions a population violates.
- Compare observed and expected genotype frequencies.
- Distinguish equilibrium tests from allele-frequency comparisons.

=== layout: section

# Conditions for Hardy-Weinberg

=== layout: one-panel

# *Conditions for Hardy-Weinberg Equilibrium*

@body

- The Hardy-Weinberg theorem describes a hypothetical population that is not evolving
- In real populations, allele and genotype frequencies do change over time
- If alleles frequencies are close the Hardy-Weinberg values, we can hypothesize that the population is not evolving for that allele.

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme


@body

- The five conditions for non-evolving populations are rarely met in nature
  - No mutations
  - Random mating
  - No natural selection
  - Extremely large population size
  - No gene flow
- When these conditions are NOT met then it implies the population is evolving.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 1](assets/lect14d-hardy_weinberg_conditions/image_001.png)

=== layout: two-panels
@replaceme

# *The Five Hardy-Weinberg Conditions for Not Evolving*

@left

- Negative conditions
  - No mutations
  - No selective mating
  - No natural selection
  - No genetic drift
  - No gene flow

@right

- Positive conditions
  - Alleles remain same
  - Random mating
  - All individuals equally likely to survive
  - Large population size
  - Isolated community

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

For biologists studying a large flatworm population in the lab, which Hardy-Weinberg condition is [*most difficult*]{underline=true} to meet?

- A) no selection
- B) no genetic drift
- C) no gene flow
- D) no mutation

@answer

[Answer\: D]{color=red}

=== layout: multiple-choice

@question

For a biologist studying a small fish population in the lab, which Hardy-Weinberg condition is [*easiest*]{underline=true} to meet?

- A) no selection
- B) no genetic drift
- C) no gene flow
- D) no mutation

@answer

[Answer\: C]{color=red}

=== layout: section

# Assessing whether the population is in Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium

=== layout: one-panel


@body

- Researchers studying a small milkweed population note that some plants produce a toxin and other plants do not.
  - They identify the gene responsible for toxin production.
- [dominant allele (T) codes for an enzyme that makes the toxin]{color=red}
- [recessive allele (t) codes for a nonfunctional enzyme that cannot produce the toxin.]{color=gray}
  - [Heterozygotes produce an intermediate amount of toxin.]{color=#BF819E}
- The genotypes of all individuals in the population are determined (see chart) and used to determine the actual allele frequencies in the population.

=== layout: four-panels
@replaceme


