# *Method 1\: Count individual alleles and divide by total*

@body

![Slide image 4](assets/lect14d-hardy_weinberg_conditions/image_004.jpg)

- p = f(A) = 1780/2000 = 0.89 = 89%
- q = f(a) = 220/2000 = 0.11 = 11%

=== layout: one-panel
@replaceme

# *Method 2\: Only works with genotype frequencies*

@body

![Slide image 5](assets/lect14d-hardy_weinberg_conditions/image_005.jpg)

- p = f(A) = 0.795 + 0.19/2 = 0.89 = 89%
- q = f(a) = 0.015 + 0.19/2 = 0.11 = 11%

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels
@replaceme


@left

- The genotypes of all individuals in the population are determined (see chart) and used to determine the actual allele frequencies in the population.
- Method 2\:
  - p = TT + &#189;Tt = 0.56 + &#189;&#215;0.28 = 0.7
  - q = 1-p = 0.3
  - q = tt + &#189;Tt = 0.16 + 0.14 = 0.3
- Method 1\:
  - p = (56\*2 + 28)/200 = 140/200 = 0.7
  - q = (28 + 16\*2)/200 = 60/200 = 0.3

