=== layout: section

# Exceptions to Hardy-Weinberg

=== layout: one-panel

# *Exceptions to the Hardy-Weinberg Principle*

@body

- Nonrandom mating
- Unequal survival
- Population subdivision
- Migration

=== layout: one-panel

# *Nonrandom Mating*

@body

- Nonrandom mating includes
  - Consanguineous mating (mating between genetically related individuals)
  - Assortative mating (mating between individuals with similar phenotypes)
- Both consanguineous mating (inbreeding) and assortative mating (sexual selection) reduce the frequency of heterozygotes and increase the frequency of homozygotes compared to the Hardy-Weinberg genotype frequencies.

=== layout: one-panel


@body

![Slide image 10](assets/lect14d-hardy_weinberg_conditions/image_010.png)

- Remarkably, [white birds]{color=gray} of either sex mate almost exclusively with [tan birds]{color=#E8A202} of the opposite sex.
- A pattern which results in four effective &#8216;sexes&#8217;, as any given individual can breed with only one-quarter (25%) of the population

=== layout: one-panel

# *Unequal Survival*

@body

- If zygotes produced by random mating have different survival rates, the genotype frequencies of the individuals that develop from these zygotes are not expected to conform to the Hardy-Weinberg predictions.
- Example\: Consider a randomly mating _Drosophila_ population segregation two alleles, _A1_ and _A2_, of an autosomal gene.

=== layout: one-plus-two-panels
@replaceme

# *Randomly mating* *_Drosophila_* *population*

@left

![Slide image 11](assets/lect14d-hardy_weinberg_conditions/image_011.svg)

@top-right

| Genotype | Observed | H-W Expected |
| A1A1 | 26 | 46.1 |
| A1A2 | 140 | 99.8 |
| A2A2 | 34 | 54.1 |

@bottom-right

- A sample of 200 adults yielded the data in the table.
  - The expected numbers were obtained by estimating the allele frequencies and applying the Hardy-Weinberg principle.
- An excess of heterozygotes and a shortage of homozygotes is observed.
  - The discrepancy is probably due to differential survival.

=== layout: one-panel

# *Population Subdivision*

@body

- When a population is a single interbreeding unit, it is [*panmictic*]{color=#339966}.
- Panmixis implies that any member of the population is able to mate with any other member.
- In nature, populations may be subdivided due to geographical or ecological barriers that may be correlated with genetic differences.
- This violates the assumption that allele frequencies are uniform in the population.

